        <body>
            <div xml:id="d270">

                <pb facs="0561" n="437" sameAs="frus:frus1881/pg437"/>

                <head>No. 266.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Morton to Mr. Blaine</hi>.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1881-12-04"><hi rendition="#italic">December</hi> 4,
                        1881</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Just had long interview at my house with Mr. Gambetta. He says, although
                    microscopic bill withdrawn, parliamentary commission still <pb facs="0562"
                        n="438" xml:id="pg438"/> exists, and majority of said commission favors
                    retention of decree, but says if United States agree to cause governmental
                    inspection of salted meats for export, with necessary sanitary regulations, and
                    communicate same to him, he has reason to believe it will weaken the present
                    opposition in the Chambers to withdrawal of decree and secure favorable report
                    from parliamentary commission. I believe some such assurance necessary to secure
                    revocation.</p>
                <closer><signed>MORTON.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
